<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib">
  <!-- Opts (en) -->

  <!-- Categories -->
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.power">Power and CPU</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.power.desc">Power plans and CPU settings that keep your PC running at full speed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.games">Games and FPS</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.games.desc">Display, GPU and priority tweaks that make games run smoother.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.input">Mouse and keyboard</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.input.desc">More direct input response and fewer interruptions while you play.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.network">Network</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.network.desc">Lower latency and less needless traffic on your connection.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.background">Background and RAM</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.background.desc">Services, tasks and apps that quietly use memory, CPU or disk.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.interface">Windows interface</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="cat.interface.desc">Visual effects, animations and menus, tuned so Windows feels snappier.</sys:String>

  <!-- Error messages -->
  <sys:String x:Key="err.powerRange">The power plan "{0}" does not allow this value: its range is {1} to {2}. Nothing was changed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.powerPlanCreate">Windows cannot create the Ultimate Performance plan on this PC (this usually happens with Modern Standby). Use "Power mode: best performance" instead.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.powerPlanActivate">The Ultimate Performance plan could not be activated.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.powerMode">Windows refused to change the power mode (is a plan other than Balanced active?).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.refresh">Could not set {0} to {1} Hz (code {2}).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.widgets">Windows protects this setting from external programs. Turn Widgets off manually in Settings › Personalization › Taskbar › Widgets.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="err.widgetsRevert">Windows protects this setting from external programs. Turn Widgets back on in Settings › Personalization › Taskbar › Widgets.</sys:String>

  <!-- Power and CPU -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerPlan.name">"Ultimate Performance" power plan</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerPlan.desc">Switches to the hidden Windows plan that keeps the CPU at full speed with no power saving for disks or PCI Express. It can slightly lower latency and steady the frame rate, but on Windows 11 the Balanced plan already ramps up quickly, so the difference is usually small. Uses more power. On laptops, “Power mode” is a better fit.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerMode.name">Power mode: best performance</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerMode.desc">Sets the "Power mode" control in Settings › System › Power to "Best performance". This is the right choice for a plugged-in laptop. It only works with the Balanced plan and applies to the current power source (charger or battery).</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.CoreParking.name">Turn off CPU core parking</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.CoreParking.desc">Keeps every CPU core awake while plugged in. It can remove the odd micro-stutter in older games, but Windows 11 already manages cores well, so the effect is usually minimal. Do not turn it on with dual-chiplet Ryzen X3D CPUs (7900X3D, 7950X3D, 9900X3D, 9950X3D): AMD deliberately parks the chiplet without V-Cache during games and this prevents it, lowering FPS.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerThrottling.name">Turn off Power Throttling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PowerThrottling.desc">Stops Windows from slowing down background programs. It does not help gaming: those programs compete more with your game and, on CPUs with efficiency cores (Intel 12th gen or newer), Windows stops sending background work to those cores, which can hurt performance. With a high-performance plan Windows barely applies it anyway. It only makes sense if you need something in the background, such as recording with OBS, to run at full speed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Hibernate.name">Turn off hibernation</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Hibernate.desc">This frees disk space; it does not improve performance. It deletes the hibernation file (hiberfil.sys), which takes several GB, or tens of GB on PCs with lots of RAM. It also turns off Fast Startup, so booting may take a little longer, and you will no longer be able to hibernate. Only recommended when you are short on space.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PciAspm.name">PCI Express without power saving</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PciAspm.desc">Stops Windows from putting the link to your graphics card and NVMe drives to sleep between uses, which can add a tiny delay when they are needed again. The cost is slightly higher power use. Applies to the active power plan.</sys:String>

  <!-- Games and FPS -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.RefreshRate.name">Monitor at its maximum refresh rate (Hz)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.RefreshRate.desc">Many 120, 144 or 165 Hz monitors are left running at 60 Hz. This sets each display to the highest rate it supports at its current resolution: more visible frames and lower latency.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.GameMode.name">Windows Game Mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.GameMode.desc">Windows prioritizes the game in the foreground and keeps Windows Update from installing drivers or restarting while you play.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.GameDVR.name">Turn off background recording (Game DVR)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.GameDVR.desc">Turns off Xbox Game Bar capture, which can record in the background and use up GPU and disk. If you record clips with Win+Alt+R or Win+Alt+G, leave this one off.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.HAGS.name">Hardware-accelerated GPU scheduling</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.HAGS.desc">The GPU manages its own video memory: less CPU load and lower latency. It is required for DLSS 3 frame generation. Needs a modern GPU (NVIDIA GTX 10-series or newer, AMD RX 5000 or newer, Intel Arc). If you notice stutter after enabling it, turn it off.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.WindowedOpt.name">Optimizations for windowed games</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.WindowedOpt.desc">DirectX 10/11 games running in a window or borderless window use the modern flip presentation model: lower input latency, plus support for VRR (G-Sync/FreeSync) and Auto HDR.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MMCSS.name">Multimedia priority for games (MMCSS)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MMCSS.desc">Raises the priority the Windows multimedia scheduler gives to the “Games” task and reserves less CPU for background work. It only affects games that register with that scheduler, which are very few, so most games see no change. Harmless; turn it on only if you want to try it.</sys:String>

  <!-- Mouse and keyboard -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MouseAccel.name">Turn off mouse acceleration</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MouseAccel.desc">Turns off "Enhance pointer precision". The cursor moves exactly as far as your hand does, with no acceleration, so aim stays consistent and muscle memory stays reliable. If you only use a touchpad, you may prefer to leave it on. Takes effect immediately.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.KeyRepeat.name">Faster keyboard response</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.KeyRepeat.desc">Shortest repeat delay (250 ms instead of 500 ms) and fastest repeat rate (about 30 per second): a held key starts repeating sooner and repeats faster. Takes effect immediately.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.AccessKeys.name">Turn off Filter Keys and accessibility shortcuts</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.AccessKeys.desc">Turns off Filter Keys (which can ignore or delay quick key presses) and the Sticky Keys (Shift pressed 5 times) and Toggle Keys shortcuts, whose pop-ups interrupt games. If you use Sticky Keys on purpose, it keeps working: only the shortcut is removed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.UsbSuspend.name">Turn off USB selective suspend</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.UsbSuspend.desc">Stops Windows from putting the USB ports used by your mouse, keyboard or headset to sleep, which can cause lag on wake-up or random disconnects. On laptops it only applies while plugged in.</sys:String>

  <!-- Network -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Nagle.name">Lower TCP latency (turn off Nagle's algorithm)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Nagle.desc">Sends small TCP packets immediately instead of batching them. It only helps in games that use TCP without already disabling this themselves (some MMOs and strategy games); most action games use UDP and will not notice anything. Download speed is not affected.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.DeliveryOpt.name">Turn off Windows Update peer-to-peer sharing</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.DeliveryOpt.desc">Stops Windows from using your connection to send updates to other PCs (Delivery Optimization). Frees up upload bandwidth and disk; updates still download normally.</sys:String>

  <!-- Background and RAM -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.BgApps.name">Block Store apps in the background</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.BgApps.desc">Stops Microsoft Store apps (Photos, News, Weather, Mail...) from running in the background and using RAM and CPU. Some Store apps will stop showing notifications while they are closed.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.EdgeBg.name">Edge without preloading or background mode</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.EdgeBg.desc">Stops Microsoft Edge from preloading at startup and from staying open in the background after you close it. Saves 150-400 MB of RAM, but if Edge is your main browser it will take a little longer to open, so in that case it is not recommended. Edge will show “Your browser is managed by your organization”; that is normal when policies are used.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Widgets.name">Turn off Widgets</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Widgets.desc">Removes the Widgets panel (news and weather) from the taskbar along with its WebView processes, which take up RAM even if you never open it. If Windows protects the policy, the button is at least hidden, which is what keeps those processes running.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Tips.name">No tips, ads or promoted apps</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Tips.desc">Turns off tips, Start menu suggestions, ads and the silent installation of promoted apps. Fewer downloads and background processes.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Telemetry.name">Turn off telemetry and its tasks</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Telemetry.desc">Turns off the telemetry service (DiagTrack), the WAP push message router and the scheduled data-collection tasks, such as CompatTelRunner, which causes CPU and disk spikes. It does not affect Windows Update or the Store.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.SysMain.name">Turn off SysMain (Superfetch)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.SysMain.desc">SysMain preloads your most-used apps into RAM so they open faster. Turning it off gives no extra FPS and can make apps a little slower to open, so it is not recommended. It only makes sense if you see disk-usage spikes caused by it. With a hard disk drive (HDD), keep it on.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.WSearch.name">Turn off the search indexer</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.WSearch.desc">The indexer scans your files in the background. Turning it off frees CPU and disk, but file search in Start and File Explorer gets slower (and Outlook can no longer search). Recommended only with a mechanical hard drive.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.ActivityHistory.name">Turn off activity history</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.ActivityHistory.desc">Windows stops recording which apps and files you open and stops sending that history to Microsoft. You lose the Timeline feature and suggestions based on recent activity. Less personal data shared and a little less background work.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.UnusedServices.name">Turn off services almost nobody uses</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.UnusedServices.desc">Turns off four services almost nobody needs: offline maps (MapsBroker), fax, retail demo mode (RetailDemo) and Windows Media Player network sharing. They are almost never running, so the saving is minimal. If you use any of those features, leave this off.</sys:String>

  <!-- Windows interface -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Animations.name">Remove animations</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Animations.desc">Removes window, menu and taskbar animations so everything appears instantly. You will notice it on modest PCs (integrated graphics or little RAM); on a powerful PC it is a matter of taste.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Transparency.name">Remove transparency effects</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Transparency.desc">Removes the glass effect (Mica and acrylic) from the taskbar, the Start menu and windows. Saves some GPU, especially with integrated graphics.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MenuDelay.name">Faster menus</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MenuDelay.desc">Submenus open after 50 ms instead of 400 ms when you hover over them.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.SearchWeb.name">Start search without web results</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.SearchWeb.desc">Removes Bing suggestions and web results from Start menu search, so searches are faster and only show your apps and files. Takes effect the next time you sign in.</sys:String>
  <!-- Optimizations added in 1.4 -->
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PrioritySeparation.name">More CPU for the foreground game</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.PrioritySeparation.desc">Windows shares the CPU giving more priority and shorter turns to the program in front of you (the game). It helps keep FPS steady when things run in the background.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.FullscreenOpt.name">True exclusive fullscreen</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.FullscreenOpt.desc">Turns off Windows «fullscreen optimizations» so fullscreen games run in exclusive mode. In some DirectX 9/11 games it lowers input lag; in others it changes nothing. Alt+Tab may take a little longer.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Mpo.name">Disable multiplane overlay (MPO)</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.Mpo.desc">Fixes stutters, flickering and black screens some graphics cards (mainly NVIDIA) have with several monitors or windowed games. If you do not have those problems, you do not need it.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MemoryIntegrity.name">Turn off memory integrity</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.MemoryIntegrity.desc">Memory integrity (Windows Security &gt; Core isolation) protects the Windows kernel against malware, but it can cost 5 to 15 % FPS, especially on processors from before 2019.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.CpuMitigations.name">Remove Spectre and Meltdown protections</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="opt.CpuMitigations.desc">Turns off the processor protections against the Spectre and Meltdown flaws. On older processors (Intel 8th gen or earlier) it can give noticeably more performance; on modern ones it barely changes anything.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="risk.security">Lowers Windows protection: malicious software would have an easier time taking over the PC. Use it only on a gaming PC where you do not install programs from dubious sources. It can be undone at any time.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="risk.features">Some Windows features stop working or get slower while it is on. It can be undone at any time.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ui.badge.risk">Risk</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ui.risk.title">Turn on «{0}»?</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ui.risk.undo">Velox keeps how it was: you can turn it off whenever you want or use «Undo everything» in Tools.</sys:String>
  <sys:String x:Key="ui.risk.accept">I understand the risk</sys:String>
</ResourceDictionary>
